+/*
+ * The driver has the form of dlls/wineoss.drv/oss.c: the local buffer of
+ * mmdevapi, and a timer loop that signals the program each period. The
+ * output is an AAudio stream (libaaudio of the NDK, Android 8 and later)
+ * with a data callback: AAudio asks for each burst, and the callback takes
+ * the frames from the local buffer, as the render callback of
+ * winecoreaudio does. When the local buffer has too few frames, the rest of
+ * the burst is silence. A write mode is not possible: an AAudio stream of
+ * the mode none (an AudioTrack) plays only after its buffer is full, and a
+ * program of mmdevapi keeps only a few periods in the local buffer.
+ *
+ * The period is 10 ms, or one burst of the device when a burst is longer
+ * (see aaudio_get_device_period), so the local buffer has the frames of a
+ * burst at each callback.
+ *
+ * The output is the one output device of Android: AAudio sends the stream
+ * to the device of the route of the system. The driver has no capture
+ * device and no MIDI device.
+ *
+ * The formats: AAudio takes 16 bit PCM and 32 bit float. The driver gives
+ * 8 bit PCM to AAudio as 16 bit PCM, and 24 bit and 32 bit PCM as float. It
+ * applies the volume of mmdevapi in the same step. AAudio converts the
+ * sample rate.
+ *
+ * The performance mode: the driver asks for AAUDIO_PERFORMANCE_MODE_LOW_LATENCY
+ * first. When that stream has another sample rate, another channel count or
+ * another format than the program asked for, the driver opens a stream with
+ * the mode none. When AAudio disconnects the stream (a change of the route),
+ * the timer loop opens a new one.
+ *
+ * Each stream writes one line to the log (MESSAGE): the format of the
+ * program, what AAudio gives (the mode, the burst, the buffer), after 0.5 s
+ * of output the latency from the timestamp of AAudio, and at most each 10 s
+ * the new underruns of AAudio and the frames of silence that the callback
+ * gave while the stream played.
+ */
+
+#if 0
+#pragma makedep unix
+#endif
